---
// Gaming on Pulsar: what ships, what is tuned and why, and what doctor watches.
// Facts from system_files/usr/share/pulsar/flatpaks.list, etc/gamemode.ini,
// usr/lib/sysctl.d/60-pulsar-gaming.conf, usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/60-pulsar-gaming.conf,
// udev/rules.d/70-ntsync.rules, zz0-pulsar.gschema.override (check-alive-timeout),
// the gamemode/mangohud/ntsync/flatpak-gl checks in cli/pulsar, and the
// nvidia image's pulsar-gl-nvidia units. The doctor lines are real output.
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'What Pulsar does for games: the launchers, gamemode set up for two GPUs, ntsync for Proton, kernel tuning, and the NVIDIA driver for Flatpak games.';

const DOCTOR = [
  '$ pulsar doctor ntsync gamemode mangohud flatpak-gl',
  'ok    ntsync         /dev/ntsync present',
  '                     crw-rw-r-- root:root',
  'ok    gamemode       proto is in gamemode; renice=10 available',
  '                     igpu governor demotion disabled (igpu_power_threshold=-1)',
  'warn  mangohud       flatpaks.list pins //25.08, Steam runs on 26.08',
  '                     installed (25.08,26.08) still covers it; repin before the old branch goes',
  'ok    flatpak-gl     running Flatpak apps have the NVIDIA 615.71.09 driver',
].join('\n');

const GAMEMODE = [
  'gamemoderun %command%          # Steam: a game’s launch options',
  'sudo pulsar setup gamemode     # enroll accounts made since first boot, then log out and in',
].join('\n');

const LAUNCHERS: [string, string][] = [
  ['Steam', 'com.valvesoftware.Steam'],
  ['Heroic', 'com.heroicgameslauncher.hgl'],
  ['Faugus Launcher', 'io.github.Faugus.faugus-launcher'],
  ['Bottles', 'com.usebottles.bottles'],
  ['ProtonPlus', 'com.vysp3r.ProtonPlus'],
  ['protontricks', 'com.github.Matoking.protontricks'],
  ['MangoHud (the Vulkan layer)', 'org.freedesktop.Platform.VulkanLayer.MangoHud'],
];

const TUNING: [string, string][] = [
  ['kernel.split_lock_mitigate = 0', 'The kernel stalls a process about 10 ms for every split lock, as a deterrent. Several Windows games split-lock constantly and turn into a slideshow under it.'],
  ['vm.max_map_count = 2147483642', 'Proton games with huge address-space appetites exhaust Fedora’s default and crash.'],
  ['transparent_hugepage/defrag = defer+madvise', 'Hugepage defragmentation is deferred to the background instead of stalling the thread that faulted, which may be the one drawing your frame. Only memory a program explicitly asked to be hugepage-backed still waits for it.'],
  ['vm.compaction_proactiveness = 0', 'No compaction on a timer. Compaction when memory is actually wanted is untouched.'],
  ['vm.min_free_kbytes = 524288', '512 MB of headroom, so a game that allocates hard gets background reclaim instead of reclaiming mid-frame itself.'],
];

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Gaming on Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/gaming', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'launchers', label: 'The launchers', level: 2 },
  { id: 'gamemode', label: 'gamemode', level: 2 },
  { id: 'proton', label: 'Proton and the kernel', level: 2 },
  { id: 'nvidia', label: 'NVIDIA and Flatpak games', level: 2 },
  { id: 'loading', label: 'Loading screens', level: 2 },
  { id: 'check', label: 'Checking it', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Gaming"
  fullTitle="Gaming on Pulsar — launchers, gamemode, Proton, NVIDIA"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/gaming"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Gaming</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Log in, open Steam, play. The rest of this page is what had to be true for that sentence to
    hold, and how to check it still does.
  </p>

  <h2 id="launchers">The launchers</h2>
  <p>
    On the first boot with a network, Pulsar installs the launchers and tools from Flathub. They are
    ordinary Flatpaks: update them in GNOME Software, remove any you don’t want, and it stays gone.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Flatpak ID</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {LAUNCHERS.map(([name, id]) => (
        <tr><td>{name}</td><td><code>{id}</code></td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    What an app cannot do from inside its sandbox lives in the image instead: gamescope, gamemode,
    MangoHud and the udev rules for controllers and headsets (<code>steam-devices</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="gamemode">gamemode</h2>
  <p>
    Pulsar’s <code>/etc/gamemode.ini</code> changes two things from the defaults, both because this
    was built for a laptop with two GPUs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No governor demotion.</strong> Left alone, gamemode compares the integrated GPU’s power
      draw with the CPU’s and, above a ratio, drops the CPU to <code>powersave</code> mid-game. That
      rule was written for machines where the integrated GPU <em>is</em> the game’s GPU. With a
      discrete card doing the rendering, it measures the wrong thing and acts on it anyway. Pulsar
      turns it off (<code>igpu_power_threshold=-1</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A priority boost.</strong> <code>renice=10</code> uses the nice grant the gamemode
      package already ships. For that, your account has to be in the <code>gamemode</code> group:
      the first boot enrolls every account that exists by then.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="gamemode" code={GAMEMODE}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    Groups are fixed when you log in, so the session in which you were enrolled does not have it
    yet. <code>pulsar doctor gamemode</code> tells those two states apart, which saves you wondering
    why nothing changed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="proton">Proton and the kernel</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ntsync</code> is loaded at boot and <code>/dev/ntsync</code> is handed to whoever is at
    the seat, so Proton can use the kernel’s own Windows-style sync instead of emulating it. Without
    it Proton falls back to esync or fsync.
  </p>
  <p>A few kernel settings, each for a reason you could name:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {TUNING.map(([s, why]) => (
        <tr><td><code>{s}</code></td><td>{why}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The scheduler is <code>scx_bpfland</code> where the kernel can load it: on a CPU with eight
    performance and sixteen efficiency cores and no SMT, stock EEVDF places threads badly. Some
    Fedora kernels cannot load any BPF scheduler; on those the machine runs stock EEVDF and{' '}
    <code>pulsar doctor</code> says so rather than warning forever.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nvidia">NVIDIA and Flatpak games</h2>
  <p>
    A Flatpak app does not see the host’s NVIDIA driver. It needs a matching GL extension from
    Flathub, and it picks that up once, when it starts. Two things on the NVIDIA image keep that
    from biting:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>pulsar-gl-nvidia.service</code> installs the extension for the running driver at boot,
      and for the next driver as soon as an update is staged, so it is already there when you
      restart into it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If Steam (or any Flatpak app) was started before its extension arrived, it runs its games on
      the integrated GPU, which on many laptops means a black window. Pulsar notices and says so:{' '}
      <strong>Restart Steam to use the NVIDIA GPU</strong>. Quit it fully, not just its window, and
      open it again.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    MangoHud has the same kind of catch: the Flatpak layer only shows up in apps on the matching
    runtime branch. When Steam moves to a new runtime and the layer does not, the overlay just
    disappears, silently. <code>pulsar doctor mangohud</code> watches for exactly that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="loading">Loading screens</h2>
  <p>
    GNOME asks every window whether it is still alive, and after five seconds without an answer
    offers to kill it. A game in the middle of a level load or a shader pass is working perfectly
    and still trips that. Pulsar raises the wait to 20 seconds as a desktop default, and while a game
    runs under gamemode it suspends the check entirely, then puts back whatever was there before.
    It is deliberately not off for good: a desktop that cannot tell you an app has hung leaves you
    only a terminal to kill it with.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check">Checking it</h2>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar doctor" code={DOCTOR}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    Playing at your screen’s real resolution under fractional scaling is its own page:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/gamescale">gamescale</a>. Everything doctor can report:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
